// The layout (DS-392): pure geometry. Nested boxes of given sizes and the edges between them go in; where the boxes
// land and how the edges run come out. Nothing in it knows what a box stands for: the scene and render project their
// content into boxes and read the places back, through an IEngine the factory hands them.

package driftspec@ds {

  // what whoever places reaches only through IEngine and IRouter: an engine, a router, or a phase of the layered
  // engine (DS-392)
  definition engine-part(x) {
    title: x is an engine of the layout, a sequence's engine, a router, or one of the layered engine's phases (DS-392, DS-464)
    if x inherits driftspec@ds@layout::IEngine
    if x inherits driftspec@ds@layout::ISequenceEngine
    if x inherits driftspec@ds@layout::ITableEngine
    if x inherits driftspec@ds@layout::IRouter
    if x = driftspec@ds@layout::Framer
    if x = driftspec@ds@layout::Layerer
    if x = driftspec@ds@layout::Orderer
    if x = driftspec@ds@layout::Positioner
    if x = driftspec@ds@layout::Layering
  }

  module layout {
    @requirement REQ-00210-1                 // the layout places boxes, knowing nothing of what they stand for (DS-392)
    @requirement REQ-00211-1                 // the layout depends on nothing of the model (DS-392)
    @requirement REQ-00212-1                 // nor of render or the scene (DS-392)
    @requirement REQ-00213-1                 // whoever places reaches the engines through their interfaces (DS-392)
    requirement REQ-00210-1 {
      tags: layout, scene, render
      status: approved
      @refines SREQ-00013-1
      title: The layout shall place nested boxes of given sizes and the edges between them, knowing nothing of what they stand for
      text:
        An engine is given boxes, each with its size or how its children are arranged, and the edges between them,
        and says where every box lands and how every edge runs. The packing engine fills shelves, fixed places and
        trees, for the scene; the layered engine layers boxes along their edges and routes them, for render's
        drawings. Whoever places reaches an engine through IEngine, from the factory that names the engines.
      rationale:
        Owner, 2026-11-24 (DS-368, DS-392): the layout engine is abstracted, so the scene and the drawings share one
        geometry and each projects its own content into it.
      verification VER-00230-1 {
        status: approved
        title: Integration tests confirm the engine the factory hands out places every box of a nested graph inside its parent and apart from its siblings, and a second placement following the first is never wider
        method: test
        tests:
          tests/integration/ds/layout/test_layout_placement.cpp::TestLayoutPlacement.EveryBoxLandsInsideItsParentApartFromItsSiblings
          tests/integration/ds/layout/test_layout_placement.cpp::TestLayoutPlacement.ASecondPlacementOfShorterBoxesFollowingTheFirstIsNeverWider
      }
    }

    requirement REQ-00211-1 {
      tags: architecture, layout
      status: approved
      @refines REQ-00210-1
      title: The layout shall depend on nothing of the model
      text:
        Boxes, sizes, arrangements, edges and identities are all an engine is given. A class of the layout that
        reached the model could place a box by what it stands for, and the geometry would no longer serve every
        drawing.
      rationale:
        Owner, 2026-11-24 (DS-366, DS-392): every module has its architecture decisions and patterns, and its rules
        check that they hold. REQ-00189-1 said it of the packer while the packer was the scene's.
      verification VER-00231-1 {
        status: approved
        title: Integration tests confirm the design of ds keeps it, and is told when a class of the layout depends on the model
        method: test
        tests:
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_engine_principles.cpp::TestEnginePrinciples.TheDesignOfDsKeepsEveryPrinciple
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_engine_principles.cpp::TestEnginePrinciples.APackerThatKnowsTheModelIsTold
      }
    }

    requirement REQ-00212-1 {
      tags: architecture, layout
      status: approved
      @refines REQ-00210-1
      title: The layout shall depend on nothing of render or the scene
      text:
        Render and the scene project their content into boxes and read the places back; the layout is what they
        reach, never what reaches them. A class of the layout that knew a diagram's IR or the scene's document
        would place for one of them, and the geometry would no longer serve the other.
      rationale:
        Owner, 2026-11-24 (DS-368, DS-392): layout knows no model, no render and no scene.
      verification VER-00232-1 {
        status: approved
        title: Integration tests confirm the design of ds keeps it, and is told when a class of the layout depends on render or on the scene
        method: test
        tests:
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_engine_principles.cpp::TestEnginePrinciples.TheDesignOfDsKeepsEveryPrinciple
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_engine_principles.cpp::TestEnginePrinciples.ALayoutThatKnowsRenderOrTheSceneIsTold
      }
    }

    requirement REQ-00213-1 {
      tags: architecture, layout
      status: approved
      @refines REQ-00210-1
      title: Whoever places shall reach an engine only through IEngine and a router only through IRouter, from the factories that name them
      text:
        The scene and render ask EngineFactory for an engine, and the layered engine is handed its router by
        RouterFactory. Neither holds a concrete engine, a router or a phase of the layered engine, so an engine
        or a router can be added or changed without either of them knowing.
      rationale:
        Owner, 2026-11-24 (DS-368, DS-392): whoever places talks to the engine only through the interface.
      verification VER-00233-1 {
        status: approved
        title: Integration tests confirm the design of ds keeps it, and is told when the scene or render depends on an engine, a router or a phase of the layered engine
        method: test
        tests:
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_engine_principles.cpp::TestEnginePrinciples.TheDesignOfDsKeepsEveryPrinciple
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_engine_principles.cpp::TestEnginePrinciples.APlacerThatHoldsAnEngineOrAPhaseIsTold
      }
    }

    rule layout-knows-no-model {
      title: no class of the layout depends on the model, so an engine places boxes from their sizes and arrangements alone
      @requirement REQ-00211-1
      for class c in driftspec@ds@layout
      never depends(c, x), x in driftspec@ds@model
    }

    rule layout-knows-no-render {
      title: no class of the layout depends on render, so render reaches the geometry and the geometry serves every drawing
      @requirement REQ-00212-1
      for class c in driftspec@ds@layout
      never depends(c, x), x in driftspec@ds@render
    }

    rule layout-knows-no-scene {
      title: no class of the layout depends on the scene, so the scene reaches the geometry and never the other way
      @requirement REQ-00212-1
      for class c in driftspec@ds@layout
      never depends(c, x), x in driftspec@ds@scene
    }

    rule the-scene-places-through-iengine {
      title: no class of the scene depends on an engine, a router or a phase of the layered engine, so it places through IEngine
      @requirement REQ-00213-1
      for class c in driftspec@ds@scene
      never depends(c, x), engine-part(x)
    }

    rule render-places-through-iengine {
      title: no class of render depends on an engine, a router or a phase of the layered engine, so it places through IEngine
      @requirement REQ-00213-1
      for class c in driftspec@ds@render
      never depends(c, x), engine-part(x)
    }

    // a layout engine: nested sized boxes and the edges between them in, where they land and how the edges run out
    class IEngine {
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + ~IEngine()
      + abstract place(graph: Graph) -> Placement
    }

    // the one place that names the engines; whoever places is handed an IEngine
    class EngineFactory {
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + static packing() -> std::unique_ptr<IEngine>
      + static layered(spacing: Spacing, router: std::unique_ptr<IRouter>) -> std::unique_ptr<IEngine>   // orthogonal when handed none
      + static sequence(spacing: SequenceSpacing) -> std::unique_ptr<ISequenceEngine>   // the stacked one, in the sink's spacing (DS-464)
      + static table(spacing: TableSpacing) -> std::unique_ptr<ITableEngine>   // the grid one, in the sink's spacing (DS-464)
    }

    sequence EngineFactory::packing {
      local engine: PackingEngine
      return engine
    }

    sequence EngineFactory::layered {
      local engine: LayeredEngine
      return engine
    }

    sequence EngineFactory::sequence {
      local engine: StackedEngine
      return engine
    }

    sequence EngineFactory::table {
      local engine: GridEngine
      return engine
    }

    // ---- a table's engine (DS-464): columns, rows and the lines of each cell, placed once with the sink's measures ----

    // a table engine: a table graph in, where its columns, rows and lines land out
    class ITableEngine {
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + ~ITableEngine()
      + abstract place(graph: TableGraph) -> TablePlacement
    }

    // each text broken into lines no longer than the wrap, between words; each column as wide as its widest line and
    // its room each side; each row as tall as its tallest cell, or its least
    class GridEngine : ITableEngine {
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      - owns spacing_: TableSpacing
      + GridEngine(spacing: TableSpacing)
      + place(graph: TableGraph) -> TablePlacement
      - breaks(text: TableText) -> std::vector<std::size_t>       // the word each line after the first starts with
      - widest(text: TableText, breaks: std::vector<std::size_t>) -> std::size_t
    }

    // the distances a table is placed with, in the sink's units: the svg's proportions, across in characters and down
    // in lines; a cell wraps at the same characters in every sink
    dataclass TableSpacing {
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + advance: int
      + padX: int
      + headHeight: int
      + rowHeight: int
      + lineHeight: int
      + linePad: int
      + wrap: std::size_t
      + static of(advance: int, lineHeight: int) -> TableSpacing
    }

    // a table to place, knowing nothing of what it stands for: its corner, its headings and its rows
    dataclass TableGraph {
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + owns corner: TableText
      + owns many columns: std::vector<TableText>
      + owns many rows: std::vector<TableRow>
    }

    dataclass TableRow {                     // a row's label and its cells, column by column
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + owns label: TableText
      + owns many cells: std::vector<TableText>
    }

    dataclass TableText {                    // a text's words, each in characters
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + words: std::vector<std::size_t>
    }

    // where a table lands, in the sink's units: each column's edge and width, the headings' height, each row's top and
    // height, and where each text breaks into lines, in reading order
    dataclass TablePlacement {
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + columnX: std::vector<int>
      + columnWidth: std::vector<int>
      + headHeight: int
      + rowY: std::vector<int>
      + rowHeight: std::vector<int>
      + breaks: std::vector<std::vector<std::size_t>>
      + width: int
      + height: int
    }

    // ---- a sequence's engine (DS-464): lanes, rows and frames, placed once with the sink's measures ----------------

    // a sequence engine: a sequence graph in, where its lanes, rows and frames land out
    class ISequenceEngine {
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + ~ISequenceEngine()
      + abstract place(graph: SequenceGraph) -> SequencePlacement
    }

    // lanes side by side, wide enough for their heads and the labels that cross them, a label's lack of room spread
    // over the gaps it crosses; a row per message, a lead above it for each frame that opens there and each else; frames
    // around the rows they span, those that open together outer first, nested ones further out
    class StackedEngine : ISequenceEngine {
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      - owns spacing_: SequenceSpacing
      + StackedEngine(spacing: SequenceSpacing)
      + place(graph: SequenceGraph) -> SequencePlacement
    }

    // the distances a sequence is placed with, in the sink's units: proportions written once, across in characters and
    // down in lines, scaled by the sink's measures and rounded; the SVG's own, so its 8 px and 18 px give its pixels
    dataclass SequenceSpacing {
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + laneGap: int
      + headPad: int
      + actorPad: int
      + crossPad: int
      + selfWidth: int
      + selfLead: int
      + selfTail: int
      + edge: int
      + marginX: int
      + frameInset: int
      + frameSide: int
      + selfFrameTail: int
      + frameLabelLead: int
      + frameLabelGap: int
      + frameLabelTail: int
      + advance: int
      + smallAdvance: int                    // a character of the small text a frame's label is written in
      + marginY: int
      + headHeight: int
      + headGap: int
      + rowHeight: int
      + selfHeight: int
      + lineOffset: int
      + frameLead: int
      + frameGap: int
      + dividerLead: int
      + frameBottom: int
      + bottomPad: int
      + dividerOffset: int
      + static of(advance: int, lineHeight: int, smallAdvance: int, least: SequenceLeast) -> SequenceSpacing
      + kLine: double                        // the SVG's 18 px line the proportions are written in (DS-477)
    }

    // the least room a sink's marks need in a sequence, in its units: around a head's label, a head's height, a message
    // to its own lane, and between a frame's edge and the next; the spacing takes the larger of it and its proportion
    dataclass SequenceLeast {
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + headPad: int
      + headHeight: int
      + selfHeight: int
      + frameGap: int
    }

    // a sequence to place, knowing nothing of what it stands for: its lanes, its messages by row, its frames
    dataclass SequenceGraph {
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + owns many lanes: std::vector<SequenceLane>
      + owns many messages: std::vector<SequenceMessage>
      + owns many blocks: std::vector<SequenceBlock>
    }

    dataclass SequenceLane {                 // a head's label, in characters, and whether it is an actor's
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + labelLength: std::size_t
      + actor: bool
    }

    dataclass SequenceMessage {              // the lanes it runs between, its label in characters, its row
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + from: std::size_t
      + to: std::size_t
      + labelLength: std::size_t
      + row: int
    }

    dataclass SequenceBlock {                // a frame: the rows it spans, its kind and condition in characters, its elses' rows
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + first: int
      + last: int
      + classifierLength: std::size_t
      + labelLength: std::size_t
      + dividers: std::vector<int>
    }

    // where a sequence lands, in the sink's units: a place per lane, per row and per frame, its size, and the
    // proportions its marks keep
    dataclass SequencePlacement {
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + owns many lanes: std::vector<LanePlace>
      + owns many rows: std::vector<RowPlace>
      + owns many blocks: std::vector<BlockPlace>
      + width: int
      + height: int
      + headTop: int
      + headHeight: int
      + selfWidth: int
    }

    dataclass LanePlace {                    // a lane's centre and its head's width
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + centre: int
      + head: int
    }

    dataclass RowPlace {                     // a row's top, the line its arrow runs on, its bottom
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + top: int
      + line: int
      + bottom: int
    }

    dataclass BlockPlace {                   // a frame's four sides and its elses within the rows drawn
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + top: int
      + bottom: int
      + left: int
      + right: int
      + owns many dividers: std::vector<DividerPlace>
    }

    dataclass DividerPlace {                 // which of its frame's elses, and the y of its line
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + index: std::size_t
      + y: int
    }

    // render's engine (DS-185): in every layered frame, bases above what inherits and wholes above parts, components
    // packed towards a readable aspect, routes orthogonal through lanes; a frame wider than the graph's width wraps its
    // layers onto further lines. It computes in whole units, which the doubles of a graph and a placement carry exactly
    class LayeredEngine : IEngine {
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      - owns spacing_: Spacing
      - owns router_: std::unique_ptr<IRouter>
      + LayeredEngine(spacing: Spacing, router: std::unique_ptr<IRouter>)
      + place(graph: Graph) -> Placement
    }

    // ---- the layered engine's phases (DS-370): a framer per placement, the layerer, the orderer and the positioner
    // per component, and a router for the edges ------------------------------------------------------------------

    // lays out the frames of one graph: every layered frame its own kids, a component at a time, packed in rows towards
    // a readable aspect; then every box gets its place on the page and the router what the layering left
    class Framer {
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      - refs many nodes_: std::vector<const Frame*>
      - refs edges_: std::vector<Edge>
      - S: Spacing
      - maxWidth_: int
      - number_: std::vector<std::size_t>
      - nodeOf_: std::vector<int>
      - count_: std::size_t
      - rel_: std::vector<Rect>
      - parent_: std::vector<int>
      - children_: std::vector<std::vector<int>>
      - roots_: std::vector<int>
      - ways_: std::map<size_t, std::vector<Waypoint>>
      - wayFrame_: std::map<size_t, int>
      - bands_: std::map<int, Band>
      - bandFrame_: std::map<int, int>
      - lanes_: bool                         // whether the router run needs lanes in the gaps (DS-443)
      + Framer(graph: Graph, s: Spacing)
      + run(router: IRouter) -> Placement
      - nodeAt(number: std::size_t) -> int
      - ancestorIn(node: int, frameParent: int) -> int
      - frame(parent: int, available: int) -> void
      - layered(c: Component, available: int) -> void
    }

    // assigns a component's layers: cycles broken in a walk over the written order, longest paths from the sources,
    // a dummy in every layer a long edge crosses
    class Layerer {
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      - owns spacing_: Spacing
      + Layerer(spacing: Spacing)
      + of(c: Component, rel: std::vector<Rect>) -> Layering
    }

    // orders each layer to cross less: barycentre sweeps down and up, stable so the written order breaks ties
    class Orderer {
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + static order(layering: Layering) -> void
    }

    // places a layered component: rows with lanes, nodes pulled under their neighbours, layers wrapped to the room
    class Positioner {
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      @requirement REQ-00251-1               // without lanes every gap keeps gapY (DS-443)
      - owns spacing_: Spacing
      - lanes_: bool
      + Positioner(spacing: Spacing, lanes: bool)
      + place(layering: Layering, available: int) -> void
      + apply(layering: Layering, c: Component, rel: std::vector<Rect>) -> void
    }

    // how the edges of a layered drawing run: one way of routing, given the boxes and what the layering left
    class IRouter {
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + ~IRouter()
      + abstract route(routing: Routing) -> std::map<std::size_t, Route>
      + abstract lanes() -> bool             // whether its routes need a lane for every jog between two layers (DS-443)
    }

    // routes orthogonal through the layers: anchors spread along the sides, long edges down their channels, every jog
    // a lane of its own in the gap it turns in
    class OrthogonalRouter : IRouter {
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      - owns spacing_: Spacing
      + OrthogonalRouter(spacing: Spacing)
      + route(routing: Routing) -> std::map<std::size_t, Route>
      + lanes() -> bool
    }

    // routes as smooth curves, a state diagram's (DS-443): through the layering's waypoints, borders facing the next
    // point, edges between the same boxes bowed apart, a loop beside the right side, each label on its curve off the
    // boxes; it needs no lanes
    class CurvedRouter : IRouter {
      @requirement REQ-00251-1
      - owns spacing_: Spacing
      + CurvedRouter(spacing: Spacing)
      + route(routing: Routing) -> std::map<std::size_t, Route>
      + lanes() -> bool
      - kLabelRise: int                 // how far a label's text rises above its point (DS-454)
      - static centreOf(r: Rect) -> P             // P: a point, std::pair<double, double>
      - static borderTowards(r: Rect, to: P) -> P
      - static encloses(outer: Rect, inner: Rect) -> bool
      - static crosses(r: Rect, p: P, margin: int) -> bool
      - static halfway(points: std::vector<P>) -> P
    }

    // the one place that names the routers
    class RouterFactory {
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + static orthogonal(spacing: Spacing) -> std::unique_ptr<IRouter>
      + static curved(spacing: Spacing) -> std::unique_ptr<IRouter>
      + static of(kind: std::string, spacing: Spacing) -> std::unique_ptr<IRouter>   // a state diagram's curved, any other's orthogonal (DS-443)
    }

    sequence RouterFactory::curved {
      local router: CurvedRouter
      return router
    }

    sequence RouterFactory::orthogonal {
      local router: OrthogonalRouter
      return router
    }

    dataclass Layering {                     // one component layered: what the layerer works out and the orderer and the positioner read and add to
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + real: int
      + local: std::map<int, int>
      + widths: std::vector<int>
      + heights: std::vector<int>
      + layer: std::vector<int>
      + depth: int
      + succA: std::vector<std::vector<int>>
      + predA: std::vector<std::vector<int>>
      + chain: std::map<std::pair<int, int>, std::vector<int>>
      + total: int
      + rows: std::vector<std::vector<int>>
      + rowY: std::vector<int>
      + rowH: std::vector<int>
      + gapBelow: std::vector<int>
      + x: std::vector<int>
      + minX: int
      + lineOf: std::vector<int>
      + lineTop: std::vector<int>
      + lineHeight: std::vector<int>
    }

    dataclass Component {                    // the layered placement of one connected component of a frame's kids
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + source: int                           // a constant: the pseudo node above
      + sink: int                             // a constant: the pseudo node below
      + nodes: std::vector<int>
      + owns many edges: std::vector<Projected>
      + ways: std::map<std::size_t, std::vector<Waypoint>>
      + bands: std::map<int, Band>
      + w: int
      + h: int
    }

    dataclass Projected {                    // an edge projected onto the kids of a frame: which kid is drawn above which
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + upper: int
      + lower: int
      + edge: std::size_t
    }

    dataclass Waypoint {                     // a point a long edge passes between two layers: a dummy's x and its layer's band
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + x: int
      + top: int
      + bottom: int
      + gapBelow: int
    }

    dataclass Band {                         // the gap under a box's layer, where its jogs live
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + top: int
      + bottom: int
    }

    dataclass Routing {                      // what a router is given: the boxes, the edges' ends, and what the layering left
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + owns many boxes: std::vector<Rect>
      + ends: std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>
      + ways: std::map<std::size_t, std::vector<Waypoint>>
      + bands: std::map<int, Band>
    }

    dataclass Point {                        // a point of the layered engine, in whole units
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + x: int
      + y: int
    }

    dataclass Rect {                         // a rectangle of the layered engine, in whole units
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + x: int
      + y: int
      + w: int
      + h: int
    }

    dataclass Spacing {                      // the room the layered engine leaves, in pixels or in cells
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + channel: int                          // the width a long edge runs down; a container is never narrower than eight
      + gapX: int
      + gapY: int
      + lane: int                             // one row per edge that turns between two layers
      + lanePad: int
      + containerPad: int
      + containerLabelH: int
      + aspect: double
    }

    // the scene's engine (DS-389): a frame is fixed, fills shelves or is a tree, and a graph can hold its shelves to
    // the widths an earlier placement kept. It routes no edge
    class PackingEngine : IEngine {
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + place(graph: Graph) -> Placement
      - size(frame: Frame, graph: Graph, out: Placement) -> Packed
      - fixed(frame: Frame, graph: Graph, out: Placement) -> Packed
      - shelves(frame: Frame, graph: Graph, out: Placement) -> Packed
      - tree(frame: Frame, graph: Graph, out: Placement) -> Packed
      - static width(id: std::string, computed: double, graph: Graph) -> double
      - static shelf(kids: std::vector<Packed>, gap: double, target: double, at: std::vector<std::pair<double, double>>) -> std::pair<double, double>
      - static target(kids: std::vector<Packed>, atLeast: double, factor: double) -> double
      - static land(frame: Frame, packed: Packed, x: double, y: double, out: std::vector<Box>) -> void
    }

    dataclass Graph {                        // what an engine is given: the boxes it refers to, numbered in the order it is read, the edges, how wide it may be
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + refs root: Frame
      + edges: std::vector<Edge>
      + maxWidth: double
      + widths: std::map<std::string, double>   // kept by an earlier placement, for this one to follow
      + following: bool
    }

    dataclass Placement {                    // where the boxes landed, how the edges run, and the widths kept
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + boxes: std::vector<Box>                // by number, in the order the graph is read: the root, then each frame, its kids and its ports
      + routes: std::map<std::size_t, Route>
      + widths: std::map<std::string, double>
    }

    dataclass Edge {                         // an edge between two boxes, by their numbers in the order the graph is read: whether it points up is all an engine reads
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + from: std::size_t                     // none when the end is no box of the graph
      + to: std::size_t
      + upward: bool                          // the to box sits above the from box: a base above what inherits
      + none: std::size_t                     // a constant: the end of an edge that is no box of the graph
    }

    dataclass Route {                        // how an edge runs: the corners of its polyline, in order, or the points a curve passes
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      @requirement REQ-00251-1
      + points: std::vector<std::pair<double, double>>
      + shape: Shape                         // polyline or curve (DS-443)
      + label: std::optional<std::pair<double, double>>   // where its label goes, when the router chose
    }

    dataclass Box {                          // where a box lands: its top left corner, its width and its height
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + x: double
      + y: double
      + w: double
      + h: double
    }

    dataclass Frame {                        // a box to pack (DS-389): its identity, how its children are arranged, and their frames
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + id: std::string
      + arrangement: Arrangement               // fixed, in shelves, a tree, or layered along its edges
      + kids: std::vector<Frame>
      + w: double
      + h: double
      + at: std::vector<std::pair<double, double>>
      + gap: double
      + atLeast: double
      + factor: double
      + left: double
      + top: double
      + right: double
      + bottom: double
      + ports: std::vector<Frame>
      + portTop: double
      + portGap: double
      + drop: double
      + label: double                         // a layered frame: the width its label takes
    }

    dataclass Packed {                       // a frame packed (DS-389): its size and where each child sits, from its top left corner
      @requirement REQ-00210-1
      + w: double
      + h: double
      + at: std::vector<std::pair<double, double>>
      + kids: std::vector<Packed>                 // its kids, then its ports
    }
  }
}
